  "title": {
    "en": "Pipits and wagtails"
  },
  "subtitle": {
    "en": "Meadow, Tree, Rock, Water and Red-throated Pipit, with Yellow, Grey and White Wagtail"
  },
  "intro": {
    "en": "Pipits look alike and sound different. Wagtails look different and sound alike, almost. In both groups, the call helps more than the plumage, and a good birder can name a pipit in the dark. The streaking on the breast and flanks, the colour of the legs and the white on the tail are the other clues.\n\nThe pipits of today are from three habitats: Meadow Pipit (open ground), Tree Pipit (woods and heath) and Rock Pipit (rocky shore), with the winter Water Pipit and the rarer Red-throated Pipit. Wagtails are much easier, but the Yellow Wagtail comes in many forms that differ by region and have different head colours.\n\nSeason: Tree Pipits arrive in mid-April and leave in August to September, passing through the coast in a rush. Water Pipits spend the winter on watercress beds and flooded fields, from October to March. Yellow Wagtails are summer visitors, April to September."
  },
  "words": [
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "streaking"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "The dark lines on the breast and flanks. Their width and sharpness differ in each pipit."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "hind claw"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "The claw of the back toe. Long and nearly straight in a Meadow Pipit, short and curved in a Tree Pipit."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "outer tail feathers"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "The outermost feathers of the tail. They are white in pipits and wagtails and show in flight."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "head pattern"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "The pattern of eyebrow, cheek and crown on a bird's head."
      }
    }
  ],
